---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>3.4 — Comparing Objects &amp; The CommandParser Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topic 2.6 · 90-Minute Unified Build.</strong> On the AP Computer Science A exam,
    you never write interactive <code>Scanner</code> loops on Free Response questions. Instead,
    you write methods that accept <code>String</code> and object parameters, guard against
    <code>null</code> references, slice strings using only the
    <a href="../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick Reference</a> methods
    (<code>equals</code>, <code>compareTo</code>, <code>indexOf</code>, <code>substring</code>,
    <code>length</code>), and return exact computed codes.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Starting Files (Download Both):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/CommandParser.java" download>CommandParser.java</a> —
            bare-minimum compiling stub (method signatures and dummy returns only).
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/CommandParserCheck.java" download>CommandParserCheck.java</a> —
            automated AP-style test suite (38 checks across 4 parts).
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; margin-bottom:0;">
        Compile and run from your terminal right now to verify your setup:
    </p>
    <pre><code>javac CommandParser.java CommandParserCheck.java
java CommandParserCheck</code></pre>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 1 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>isSameCommand(String input, String target)</code></h3>

    <p>
        Primitives (<code>int</code>, <code>double</code>, <code>boolean</code>) store their raw value
        directly in the variable, so <code>==</code> compares their values. Objects like
        <code>String</code> store a <strong>memory reference</strong> (an address on the heap).
        Using <code>==</code> on two <code>String</code> variables asks:
        <em>"Are these two variables pointing at the exact same object in memory?"</em>
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap 1: The String Interning Illusion</h3>
        <p>
            Before writing your real solution, put this one-line body inside
            <code>isSameCommand</code> in <code>CommandParser.java</code> and run
            <code>java CommandParserCheck</code>:
        </p>
        <pre><code>public static boolean isSameCommand(String input, String target) {
    return input == target;
}</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>Look at the first two lines of output under Part 1 in <code>CommandParserCheck</code>.
                Why did <code>isSameCommand("attack", "attack")</code> PASS with <code>==</code>,
                while <code>isSameCommand("attack", new String("attack"))</code> FAILED — even though
                both strings contain the exact letters <code>"attack"</code>?</li>
            <li>Now change <code>return input == target;</code> to
                <code>return input.equals(target);</code> and re-run <code>CommandParserCheck</code>.
                The heap check passes, but the last three checks in Part 1 crash with
                <code>NullPointerException</code>! Why does calling <code>input.equals(target)</code>
                crash when <code>input</code> is <code>null</code>, and why does
                <code>input.equals(target) &amp;&amp; input != null</code> STILL crash if you put the
                null check second?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h4>AP Specification — <code>isSameCommand</code></h4>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>public static boolean isSameCommand(String input, String target)</code></li>
        <li><strong>Behavior:</strong> Returns <code>true</code> if and only if both <code>input</code>
            and <code>target</code> are non-null and contain the exact same sequence of characters
            (case-sensitive). Returns <code>false</code> if either parameter is <code>null</code> or
            their characters differ.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Fix <code>isSameCommand</code> and run <code>java CommandParserCheck</code> until all
        <strong>8 checks in Part 1</strong> report <code>PASS</code>.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 2 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>extractVerb(String rawCommand)</code> &amp; <code>extractTarget(String rawCommand)</code></h3>

    <p>
        In an RPG command like <code>"cast fireball"</code> or <code>"attack goblin"</code>, the first
        word is the <strong>verb</strong> and the text after the first space is the
        <strong>target</strong>. Single-word commands like <code>"defend"</code> or
        <code>"flee"</code> have a verb and an empty target (<code>""</code>).
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>The AP Exam Rule:</strong> Methods like <code>split()</code>, <code>startsWith()</code>,
        and <code>charAt()</code> are <strong>not</strong> on the
        <a href="../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick Reference</a>. On FRQ 1, you slice
        strings using two tools that ARE on the sheet:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>rawCommand.indexOf(" ")</code> — returns the index of the first space, or
            <code>-1</code> if there is no space in the string.</li>
        <li><code>rawCommand.substring(from, to)</code> — returns characters from index
            <code>from</code> (inclusive) to <code>to</code> (exclusive).</li>
        <li><code>rawCommand.substring(from)</code> — returns everything from index
            <code>from</code> to the end of the string.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap 2: The <code>-1</code> Crash &amp; The Leading-Space Off-By-One</h3>
        <p>
            Many students first write <code>extractVerb</code> and <code>extractTarget</code> like this:
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Naive attempt — DO NOT KEEP:
int space = rawCommand.indexOf(" ");
String verb = rawCommand.substring(0, space);
String target = rawCommand.substring(space);</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>Trace what happens when <code>rawCommand</code> is <code>"defend"</code> (no space).
                What integer does <code>rawCommand.indexOf(" ")</code> return, and what happens when
                that integer is passed into <code>rawCommand.substring(0, space)</code>?</li>
            <li>Trace what happens when <code>rawCommand</code> is <code>"attack goblin"</code>
                (where the space is at index <code>6</code>). What exact string does
                <code>rawCommand.substring(6)</code> return, and why does
                <code>extractTarget("attack goblin").equals("goblin")</code> evaluate to
                <code>false</code> unless you adjust the start index?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h4>AP Specification — <code>extractVerb</code> &amp; <code>extractTarget</code></h4>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Signatures:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><code>public static String extractVerb(String rawCommand)</code></li>
                <li><code>public static String extractTarget(String rawCommand)</code></li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Null / Empty Guard:</strong> If <code>rawCommand</code> is <code>null</code> or
            <code>rawCommand.length() == 0</code>, both methods must return <code>""</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>When a space exists</strong> (<code>int space = rawCommand.indexOf(" ");</code> is
            <code>&gt;= 0</code>):
            <ul>
                <li><code>extractVerb</code> returns the substring before the first space.</li>
                <li><code>extractTarget</code> returns the substring after the first space (if the
                    space is the very last character, such as <code>"attack "</code>, that substring
                    is naturally <code>""</code>).</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>When no space exists</strong> (<code>space == -1</code>):
            <ul>
                <li><code>extractVerb</code> returns <code>rawCommand</code> in full.</li>
                <li><code>extractTarget</code> returns <code>""</code>.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Implement both methods and run <code>java CommandParserCheck</code> until all
        <strong>10 checks in Part 2</strong> pass.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 3 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>firstInTurnOrder(String nameA, String nameB)</code></h3>

    <p>
        <code>equals</code> tells you whether two strings are identical, but it cannot tell you
        which string comes first alphabetically. For ordering, <code>String</code> provides
        <code>a.compareTo(b)</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Returns a <strong>negative integer</strong> (<code>&lt; 0</code>) if <code>a</code> comes
            <em>before</em> <code>b</code> in Unicode (dictionary) order.</li>
        <li>Returns <strong><code>0</code></strong> if <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> are equal.</li>
        <li>Returns a <strong>positive integer</strong> (<code>&gt; 0</code>) if <code>a</code> comes
            <em>after</em> <code>b</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap 3: The <code>compareTo == -1</code> Myth &amp; ASCII Case Order</h3>
        <p>
            A classic mistake is assuming <code>compareTo</code> only returns <code>-1</code>,
            <code>0</code>, or <code>1</code>, and writing:
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Broken check — why does this fail on ("Aria", "Zorg")?
if (nameA.compareTo(nameB) == -1) {
    return nameA;
}</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>When Java compares <code>"Aria".compareTo("Zorg")</code>, it subtracts the Unicode
                values of the first differing characters: <code>'A' - 'Z'</code> (<code>65 - 90</code>).
                What exact integer does <code>"Aria".compareTo("Zorg")</code> return, and why did
                <code>== -1</code> fail in <code>CommandParserCheck</code>?</li>
            <li>In Unicode, uppercase letters (<code>'A'</code>–<code>'Z'</code>) have codes
                <code>65</code>–<code>90</code>, while lowercase letters (<code>'a'</code>–<code>'z'</code>)
                have codes <code>97</code>–<code>122</code>. Between <code>"goblin"</code> and
                <code>"Boss"</code>, which string does <code>compareTo</code> say comes first, and why?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h4>AP Specification — <code>firstInTurnOrder</code></h4>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>public static String firstInTurnOrder(String nameA, String nameB)</code></li>
        <li><strong>Precondition:</strong> <code>nameA</code> and <code>nameB</code> are non-null.</li>
        <li><strong>Behavior:</strong> Returns whichever of <code>nameA</code> or <code>nameB</code>
            comes first using <code>compareTo</code>. If they are equal, returns <code>nameA</code>.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 4 · AP FRQ Synthesis Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>resolveCombatTurn(String rawCommand, int mana, int potions, boolean isShielded)</code></h3>

    <p>
        Now combine your helper methods from Parts 1–3 to build a complete AP FRQ-style command
        resolver. Instead of re-writing string slicing inside <code>resolveCombatTurn</code>,
        <strong>call your own <code>extractVerb(rawCommand)</code> and
        <code>extractTarget(rawCommand)</code></strong>!
    </p>

    <h4>Action Code Specification Table</h4>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Command Verb</th><th>Target Rule</th><th>State Condition</th><th>Return Code</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>null</code> or <code>""</code></td>
            <td>Any</td>
            <td>Command is null or empty</td>
            <td><code>-1</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>"attack"</code></td>
            <td>Target is <code>""</code> (missing)</td>
            <td>Any</td>
            <td><code>-1</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>"attack"</code></td>
            <td>Target is <code>"boss"</code></td>
            <td><code>isShielded</code> is <code>true</code> / <code>false</code></td>
            <td><code>15</code> if shielded, <code>10</code> if not</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>"attack"</code></td>
            <td>Any other non-empty target</td>
            <td>Any</td>
            <td><code>10</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>"cast"</code></td>
            <td>Target is <code>""</code> (missing)</td>
            <td>Any</td>
            <td><code>-1</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>"cast"</code></td>
            <td>Target is <code>"fireball"</code></td>
            <td>Costs <code>10</code> mana</td>
            <td><code>25</code> if <code>mana &gt;= 10</code>, else <code>0</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>"cast"</code></td>
            <td>Target is <code>"heal"</code></td>
            <td>Costs <code>5</code> mana</td>
            <td><code>20</code> if <code>mana &gt;= 5</code>, else <code>0</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>"cast"</code></td>
            <td>Any other spell where <code>target.compareTo("m") &lt; 0</code></td>
            <td>Arcane spell: costs <code>8</code> mana</td>
            <td><code>22</code> if <code>mana &gt;= 8</code>, else <code>0</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>"cast"</code></td>
            <td>Any other spell where <code>target.compareTo("m") &gt;= 0</code></td>
            <td>Mystic spell: costs <code>12</code> mana</td>
            <td><code>24</code> if <code>mana &gt;= 12</code>, else <code>0</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>"drink"</code></td>
            <td>Target must be <code>"potion"</code> (any other target returns <code>-1</code>)</td>
            <td>Requires <code>potions &gt; 0</code></td>
            <td><code>30</code> if <code>potions &gt; 0</code>, else <code>0</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>"defend"</code></td>
            <td>Target must be <code>""</code> (if non-empty, return <code>-1</code>)</td>
            <td>Any</td>
            <td><code>40</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>"flee"</code></td>
            <td>Target must be <code>""</code> (if non-empty, return <code>-1</code>)</td>
            <td>Any</td>
            <td><code>50</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Any other verb</td>
            <td>Any</td>
            <td>Unknown command</td>
            <td><code>-1</code></td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint — Ordering Your Branches</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Inside the <code>"cast"</code> branch, why must you check for specific spells
                (<code>"fireball"</code> and <code>"heal"</code>) <em>before</em> checking
                <code>target.compareTo("m") &lt; 0</code>? What would happen to
                <code>"cast fireball"</code> and <code>"cast heal"</code> if the
                <code>compareTo("m")</code> check came first?</li>
            <li>When all 38 checks pass in <code>CommandParserCheck</code>, paste your final
                <code>38 passed, 0 failed</code> confirmation line here and explain which of the
                four parts caught a bug in your first attempt.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><code>java CommandParserCheck</code> reports <strong><code>38 passed, 0 failed</code></strong></li>
            <li>You have answered the reflection boxes above and downloaded your <code>.json</code> for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

